                        "glosses": [
                            "Not engulfed or submerged; (figuratively) not overcome with emotion."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "26f4c934-afee-475c-be22-08efefe4db14",
                                "sense_id": "327a2934-e0e1-36a4-ba9c-4a4ef7a4844f",
                                "text": "1872, William Watkiss Lloyd, The History of Sicily to the Athenian War; with Elucidations of the Sicilian Odes of Pindar, London: John Murray, Book 2, Chapter 6 “To Hiero of Syracuse (Second Pythian Ode),” p. 291,\nFor I, like the cork above a net, whilst the other tackle plies the sea-toil deep below, am still unwhelmed by the brine."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "79bf8886-1dcd-4314-ae6a-2c9a9a6f7258",
                                "sense_id": "327a2934-e0e1-36a4-ba9c-4a4ef7a4844f",
                                "text": "1915, Ludwig Lewisohn (translator), Charlemagne’s Hostage, Act II, in The Dramatic Works of Gerhart Hauptmann, New York: B.W. Huebsch, Volume 5, p. 300,\n[…] The old man with the look of one\nDrowning, who gave thy freedom unto thee—\n’Tis he still breathing, still unwhelmed, who crosses\nThy path once more to-day."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "44167275-b6b3-4c74-bc92-cf40be6c46ea",
                                "sense_id": "327a2934-e0e1-36a4-ba9c-4a4ef7a4844f",
                                "text": "Under the most favourable conditions dialects are destined to dwindle and decay, and we ought to be grateful to the writers who preserve for us a little longer old-time habits, customs and idiosyncracies in the folk speech of small communities as yet unwhelmed by the onrush of modern life."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
